<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-damped-mass-spring" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>The Damped Mass-Spring System</title>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       INTRO PROSE + DASHPOT INTERACTIVE
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    In <xref ref="sec-undamped-mass-spring"/>, we modeled a mass attached to a spring
    and derived the ODE <m>mx'' + kx = 0</m>. That model assumed no friction
    whatsoever, so the mass oscillated forever. In reality, friction always acts to
    slow things down. We now add a <term>dashpot</term> to the system to model this
    frictional resistance.
  </p>

  <p>
    A dashpot is a mechanical device that resists motion: think of a piston sliding
    through a cylinder filled with fluid. The spring and dashpot both connect the mass
    to the wall, as shown in the animation below. Press Play to see how the dashpot
    changes the behavior compared to the undamped case.
  </p>

  <figure xml:id="fig-dashpot-intro">
    <caption>
      A horizontal mass-spring-dashpot system. The block of mass <m>m</m> is connected
      to the wall by a spring (top) and a dashpot (bottom). Press Play to watch the
      oscillations die out as the dashpot dissipates energy.
    </caption>
    <interactive xml:id="interactive-dashpot-intro"
      iframe="ch3-graph/interactive-dashpot-intro.html"
      width="100%" aspect="5:3">
      <description>
        An animated horizontal mass-spring-dashpot system. A block labeled m is
        attached to a fixed wall on the left by two connectors: a coiled spring on
        the upper lane and a dashpot on the lower lane. When Play is pressed, the
        block oscillates left and right with amplitude that decays over time,
        eventually coming to rest at the equilibrium position x equals 0.
      </description>
    </interactive>
  </figure>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       FORCES + DERIVING THE ODE
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    Let's derive the ODE governing this system.
    As in <xref ref="sec-undamped-mass-spring"/>, we measure position <m>x(t)</m>
    from the equilibrium position, with <m>x > 0</m> meaning the spring is stretched
    and <m>x &lt; 0</m> meaning it is compressed.
    By <xref ref="def-hookes-law"/>, the restoring force from the spring is:
    <me>F_s = -kx,</me>
    where <m>k > 0</m> is the spring constant.
    The dashpot contributes a <term>damping force</term> proportional to velocity:
    <me>F_R = -cx',</me>
    where <m>c > 0</m> is the <term>damping constant</term>. The negative sign means
    the dashpot always resists the direction of motion.
  </p>

  <p>
    By Newton's second law, the total force equals <m>mx''</m>. Since the only forces
    acting on the mass are <m>F_s</m> and <m>F_R</m>, we have:
    <me>mx'' = F_s + F_R = -kx - cx'.</me>
    Rearranging gives the <term>damped mass-spring ODE</term>:
    <me>mx'' + cx' + kx = 0.</me>
  </p>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       DEFINITION BLOCK: THE DAMPED ODE
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <definition xml:id="def-damped-mass-spring">
    <title>The Damped Mass-Spring System</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        The position <m>x(t)</m> of a mass <m>m</m> on a spring with constant
        <m>k</m> and damping constant <m>c</m> satisfies:
        <me>mx'' + cx' + kx = 0,</me>
        where <m>m, k, c > 0</m>. This is called <term>free damped motion</term>:
        <em>free</em> because there is no external driving force, and
        <em>damped</em> because the dashpot dissipates energy.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       PROSE: TYPES OF ROOTS + DISCRIMINANT PLACEHOLDER
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    To solve <m>mx'' + cx' + kx = 0</m>, we use the same characteristic equation
    approach from <xref ref="sec-all-three-cases"/>. The characteristic equation is:
    <me>mr^2 + cr + k = 0.</me>
    By the quadratic formula, the roots are:
    <me>r = \frac{-c \pm \sqrt{c^2 - 4km}}{2m}.</me>
    The expression <m>D = c^2 - 4km</m> is the <term>discriminant</term>, and its
    sign determines which of the three cases from
    <xref ref="thm-three-cases-char-eq"/> we are in.
  </p>

  <p>
    <!-- PLACEHOLDER: reference a homework exercise here where students verify
         the discriminant D = c^2 - 4km and the quadratic formula roots explicitly
         for a specific example of mx'' + cx' + kx = 0. -->
    You will practice computing these roots directly in the homework exercises.
  </p>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       MC CHECKPOINT: WHICH CASE GIVES TWO DISTINCT REAL ROOTS
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    Let's make sure we remember which sign of <m>D</m> gives which type of roots.
  </p>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mc-discriminant-overdamped">
    <statement>
      <p>
        For the characteristic equation <m>mr^2 + cr + k = 0</m>,
        which condition on <m>c</m>, <m>m</m>, and <m>k</m> gives
        two distinct real roots?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="yes">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement><p><m>c^2 - 4km > 0</m></p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct! When the discriminant is positive, the square root is real and
            nonzero, giving two distinct real roots
            <m>r = \frac{-c \pm \sqrt{c^2-4km}}{2m}</m>. <m>\checkmark</m>
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement><p><m>c^2 - 4km = 0</m></p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            When the discriminant is zero the square root vanishes, giving one
            repeated root <m>r = -c/(2m)</m>, not two distinct roots.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement><p><m>c^2 - 4km &lt; 0</m></p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            When the discriminant is negative the square root is imaginary,
            giving complex conjugate roots, not two distinct real roots.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement><p><m>c^2 - 4km \geq 0</m></p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            This includes the case <m>c^2 - 4km = 0</m>, which gives a
            repeated root, not two distinct roots. Strict inequality is needed.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       THE THREE CASES (OL)
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    In general, the three cases for the roots of <m>mr^2 + cr + k = 0</m> are:
  </p>

  <p>
    <ol>
      <li><p>
        <alert>Two distinct real roots</alert> when <m>c^2 - 4km > 0</m>:
        <me>r_1 = \frac{-c + \sqrt{c^2 - 4km}}{2m}, \qquad
           r_2 = \frac{-c - \sqrt{c^2 - 4km}}{2m}.</me>
      </p></li>
      <li><p>
        <alert>One repeated real root</alert> when <m>c^2 - 4km = 0</m>:
        <me>r = \frac{-c}{2m}.</me>
      </p></li>
      <li><p>
        <alert>Complex conjugate roots</alert> when <m>c^2 - 4km &lt; 0</m>:
        <me>r = \frac{-c}{2m} \pm i\,\frac{\sqrt{4km - c^2}}{2m}.</me>
      </p></li>
    </ol>
  </p>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       BRIDGE PROSE: EACH CASE HAS A NAME
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    As we saw in <xref ref="thm-three-cases-char-eq"/>, each type of roots leads to a
    qualitatively different form of the general solution. In the mass-spring context,
    each case also has a physical name that describes how heavily the system is damped.
    The size of the damping constant <m>c</m> relative to <m>m</m> and <m>k</m> is
    what determines which case we are in.
  </p>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       DEFINITION: THE THREE DAMPING CASES
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <definition xml:id="def-damping-cases">
    <title>The Three Damping Cases</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        For free damped motion <m>mx'' + cx' + kx = 0</m> with <m>m, k, c > 0</m>,
        the system falls into one of three cases depending on the discriminant
        <m>D = c^2 - 4km</m>:
      </p>
      <p>
        <ul>
          <li><p>
            <alert>Overdamped</alert> (<m>c^2 > 4km</m>): two distinct real roots.
            The damping constant <m>c</m> is large relative to <m>m</m> and <m>k</m>,
            so the system is slowed down the most.
          </p></li>
          <li><p>
            <alert>Critically damped</alert> (<m>c^2 = 4km</m>): one repeated real root.
            This is the borderline case, the exact value of <m>c</m> that sits between
            overdamped and underdamped.
          </p></li>
          <li><p>
            <alert>Underdamped</alert> (<m>0 &lt; c^2 &lt; 4km</m>): complex conjugate roots.
            The damping constant <m>c</m> is small, so the system still oscillates,
            just with decaying amplitude.
          </p></li>
        </ul>
      </p>
      <p>
        In all three cases <m>c > 0</m>, so the system is damped. When <m>c = 0</m>
        we recover the undamped system <m>mx'' + kx = 0</m> from
        <xref ref="sec-undamped-mass-spring"/>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       KEY TAKEAWAYS
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <conclusion>
    <title>Key Takeaways</title>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li><p>
          <alert>The ODE:</alert> Adding a dashpot gives <m>mx'' + cx' + kx = 0</m>,
          called free damped motion. The new term <m>cx'</m> models friction proportional
          to velocity.
        </p></li>
        <li><p>
          <alert>The discriminant:</alert> The sign of <m>D = c^2 - 4km</m> determines
          the type of roots of the characteristic equation and therefore the qualitative
          behavior of solutions.
        </p></li>
        <li><p>
          <alert>Three cases:</alert> Overdamped (<m>c^2 > 4km</m>), critically damped
          (<m>c^2 = 4km</m>), and underdamped (<m>0 &lt; c^2 &lt; 4km</m>). Larger <m>c</m>
          means more damping.
        </p></li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </conclusion>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       VIDEO + QUESTION FORM
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

